"""Route-goal capture scoping (exam arm 88B regression).

The capture rule: a route is captured only when the goal makes it the
subject — its name with ``endpoint``/``route``, or a standalone path
literal in a sentence that frames it as endpoint work — and never when
a path-like token merely appears inside spec prose. Arm 88B's goal,
built from freetalon/pairing/CONTRACT.md, contained the phrase
"method/path/status"; the old detector read "/status" as a named route
and the injected ROUTE GOAL shape hijacked every planning attempt into
"add a /status endpoint to api.py". The two exam goals below are the
exact runtime strings from the 88B/88C exam drivers.
"""

import unittest
from pathlib import Path

from freetalon.orchestrator.planner import (
    _route_goal_shape,
    _routes_named_in_goal,
    build_repo_index,
)

REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]

GOAL_HEALTH = (
    "Add a GET /health endpoint to FreeTalon's API that returns JSON "
    "reporting the service status, whether Ollama at localhost:11434 is "
    "reachable, and the GPU temperature when nvidia-smi is available. "
    "Add unit tests for the endpoint, keep the full test suite green, "
    "and verify the endpoint live."
)

GOAL_88B = 'Implement the freetalon.pairing package - the server side of the M6 pairing-join protocol - in this repository, exactly to the module contract below, with unit tests. The package does not exist yet; create it. Deliverables: freetalon/pairing/__init__.py, freetalon/pairing/store.py, freetalon/pairing/server.py, freetalon/pairing/__main__.py (so `python -m freetalon.pairing` starts the server), and tests in tests/test_pairing_store.py and tests/test_pairing_server.py. All of the new tests must pass with .venv/bin/python -m unittest. The store, the HTTP layer, and the tests must all answer to the contract below with ONE shared vocabulary - do not invent synonyms in different modules.\n\n== Roles ==\n- Node: a freshly installed machine on an isolated provisioning segment. It generated its pairing code itself and knows the code, its 128-bit nonce, and its machine-id. Node endpoints need no bearer token: on the isolated segment the code is a capability only ever visible on the node\'s physical console screen.\n- Operator: the control plane. Operator endpoints require `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, the token from the FREETALON_PAIRING_TOKEN environment variable only, compared with hmac.compare_digest via freetalon.security.authorize. Fail-closed: if the env var is unset or empty, every operator request is refused - no default token, no file fallback; the token is never logged and never stored in the repo.\n\n== Status vocabulary (exact strings, no synonyms) ==\npending = registered, waiting for operator confirmation. confirmed = operator confirmed; credential bundle attached, not yet claimed. claimed = bundle delivered exactly once; the code is burned. expired = TTL passed while pending or confirmed; bundle wiped. No other status string may appear anywhere.\n\n== Record fields ==\nStored per code in one JSON file: {"version": 1, "records": {...}}. Fields: code (str), nonce (str, 32 lowercase hex chars - the node\'s 128-bit nonce), machine_id (str), hostname (str|null, optional), mac (str|null, optional), status (one of the four above), credentials (object|null - the bundle; non-null ONLY while confirmed), created_at (float, epoch seconds, store clock), expires_at (float, created_at + ttl_seconds), confirmed_at (float|null), claimed_at (float|null).\nPublic view (everything the HTTP layer returns about a record): code, status, machine_id, hostname, mac, created_at, expires_at, confirmed_at, claimed_at. The public view NEVER contains nonce or credentials. The only place credentials ever leave the server is the single claiming poll.\n\n== Validation ==\n- code must match ^[A-Z]{2,}-\\d{4}$ (an uppercase word + zero-padded 4 digits, e.g. KESTREL-4821). Do not hardcode a word list.\n- nonce must be 32 hex chars; stored lowercased.\n- machine_id is required text (<=128 chars, no control characters); hostname/mac are optional text under the same rules.\n- A credential bundle must be a non-empty JSON object, JSON-serializable, <= 32768 bytes serialized. The server never inspects bundle contents.\n\n== Clock seam ==\nPairingStore(path, ttl_seconds=1800, now_fn=time.time). EVERY time read in the store goes through now_fn - nothing calls time.time directly. Tests inject a fake clock. Default TTL 1800 s, overridable via FREETALON_PAIRING_TTL_SECONDS.\n\n== Expiry ==\nLazy: on every store operation, a pending/confirmed record whose expires_at has passed becomes expired and its credentials are wiped, persisted in the same write as the operation. Claimed records never expire. Re-registering an expired code with the SAME nonce + machine-id starts a fresh pending record. Re-registering a claimed code changes nothing.\n\n== Store API (store.py) and exceptions ==\nAll errors derive from PairingError.\n- register(code, nonce, machine_id, hostname=None, mac=None) -> public view. New code -> pending. Same code + same nonce + machine_id -> idempotent, returns the current view. Same code, different nonce or machine_id -> CodeConflict. Bad input -> InvalidRegistration (also a ValueError).\n- confirm(code, credentials) -> public view. Unknown -> CodeNotFound; expired -> CodeExpired; claimed -> CodeAlreadyClaimed; already confirmed -> CodeAlreadyConfirmed; bad bundle -> InvalidBundle.\n- poll(code) -> PollOutcome(status, credentials). Unknown -> CodeNotFound (raised, never a dict). pending -> credentials None. confirmed -> returns the bundle AND burns the record in the same locked operation (status claimed, credentials wiped, claimed_at set); the outcome\'s status is "confirmed" because that is the state that was claimed. Every later poll -> "claimed", None. expired -> "expired", None.\n- list_records() -> list of public views.\n\n== Persistence ==\nSingle JSON file, one writer process. Every mutation is written atomically: temp file in the same directory opened with mode 0600, fsync, os.replace - the store file can hold live credential bundles, so it is 0600 at all times. A missing file is an empty store; a corrupt file raises StoreCorruptError (never silently reset - a reset would resurrect burned codes). All operations hold one lock; the claiming poll is atomic under it.\n\n== HTTP API (server.py) ==\nStdlib http.server, threaded. Request bodies capped at 65536 bytes (413 above that); unparseable JSON is 400. Responses are JSON. The server logs method/path/status only - never headers, never bodies, never the token.\n- POST /pairing/register (node): 201 new, 200 idempotent -> public view. Errors: 400 invalid, 409 conflict.\n- GET /pairing/<code> (node poll): pending -> 404 {"code", "status": "pending"}; unknown code -> 404 with a body identical to pending (no existence oracle); confirmed -> 200 {"code", "status": "confirmed", "credentials": {...}} exactly once; claimed -> 410 {"code", "status": "claimed"}; expired -> 410 {"code", "status": "expired"}.\n- POST /pairing/<code>/confirm (operator): 200 -> public view. Errors: 400 invalid bundle, 401 unauthorized, 404 unknown, 409 already confirmed, 410 expired or claimed.\n- GET /pairing (operator): 200 {"records": [public views]}. Error: 401 unauthorized.\n- 401 is identical for missing header, wrong token, and no token configured.\n- Unknown routes -> 404 {"error": "not found"}; a known path with the wrong method -> 405.\n\n== Configuration (env only) ==\nFREETALON_PAIRING_TOKEN (unset -> operator API closed); FREETALON_PAIRING_STORE_PATH (default /home/seeker/freetalon-workspace/pairing-store.json); FREETALON_PAIRING_TTL_SECONDS (default 1800); FREETALON_PAIRING_HOST (default 0.0.0.0); FREETALON_PAIRING_PORT (default 8080).'

